We build on our previous work in reference \cite{Melleray_Rapidly_increasing}, focusing on specific properties for sets of cardinals when considering the sum of the local regularities of these cardinals under various conditions. Notably, we define a set $\mathcal{C}$ of cardinals as 'nowhere stationary' with respect to a given ordinal $\lambda$, denoting it $\mathcal{S}(\lambda)$, if for every regular cardinal $\theta < \lambda$, the local regularity of $\mathcal{C}$ Exceeds $\theta$ in consistency. Additionally, for a set $\mathcal{C}$ of cardinals, $\mathcal{R}_{\lambda}(\mathcal{C})$ signifies the least possible regular cardinal $\mu$ where the local $\mu$-regularity of $\mathcal{C}$ holds. Considering these definitions, our results include: 
  For any $S \subseteq \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{C})$, if the average local regularity $\mathcal{R}_{\lambda}^{*}(X)$ exceeds $\lambda$ for every $X \in S$, then the average local regularity $\mathcal{R}_{\lambda}^{*}(S)$ exceeds $\lambda$ as well. 
